    {
      "openalex_id": "W4416724172",
      "doi": "10.1111/1467-8675.70026",
      "title": "Provincializing Frankfurt: A Postcolonial Rereading of Habermasian Theory",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Floris Biskamp",
          "openalex_id": "A5017895832",
          "orcid": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-8809-398X",
          "institutions": [
            "University of T\u00fcbingen"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "publication_year": 2025,
      "publication_date": "2025-11-26",
      "abstract": "The terms postcolonialism and decoloniality are used for a wide variety of critical theories that have one premise in common: the conviction that the world as we know it has been shaped by European colonialism, that it continues to be shaped by colonialism's post- or neocolonial afterlife, and that this process is violent, oppressive, and should come to an end\u2014a constellation for which Quijano (2007) coined the term coloniality. In this article, I will refer to all these approaches collectively as postcolonial critique.1 The implications postcolonial critique has for political theory and its methodology have already been discussed by various authors (e.g., Kerner 2018, 2022, 2024; Dhawan 2014). In this article, I build on their works but pursue a question that is much narrower but at the same time goes somewhat further. I not only discuss what aspects of mainstream theories appear to be \u201ccritical\u201d from a postcolonial perspective, but I also use a specific example to explain what should be done with these theories once they have been subjected to postcolonial critique: Should we still read them, and if so, how? How should they be altered? And to what extent can they still be useful? As an example for this discussion, I choose Habermasian theory. I do so for two reasons. The first reason derives from the theory itself, namely, from the proximity between postcolonial critique and critical theory (Kerner 2018, 617\u2013623). This proximity raises hope for fruitful engagement. The second reason is more subjective and idiosyncratic. Being socialized in Habermasian theory and willing to take postcolonial critique seriously, I want to find out what I should do with the critical theory I inherited. I mention this reference to a subjective and idiosyncratic motivation to avoid a possible misleading impression: I do not want to suggest that the quest for a postcolonial rereading of Habermasian theory is among the prime concerns of postcolonial critique. It is not. Nevertheless, postcolonial theory can be read with this question in mind\u2014as I argue with fruitful results. The remainder of the article consists of four sections. I begin by sketching the two aspects that seem most critical about mainstream political theory from a postcolonial perspective: first, the entanglement between the discourse of emancipation and the discourse of inequality in the Western tradition, and second, the ethnocentric treatment of global heterogeneity (Section 2). I then go on to deliver a sketch of Habermasian theory and lay out which aspects of this theory could appear critical from a postcolonial perspective (Section 3). Next, I present four types of postcolonial critique. These are the minimally invasive approach of selective historicization (Section 4.1), Amy Allen's notion of decolonization as a return to metanormative contextualism (Section 4.2), Walter Mignolo's notion of decolonization as border thinking and decoupling (Section 4.3), and a conception of provincialization found in the works of Gayatri Spivak, Dipesh Chakrabarty, and Paul Gilroy (Section 4.4). Because the latter approach calls for a thorough rereading of Western texts, the article ends with such a discussion of Habermasian theory, naming six steps that should be taken to provincialize Frankfurt and make critical theory better suited to deal with postcolonial heterogeneity (Section 5). The relationship between postcolonial critique on the one side and the different universalist theories from the Western tradition on the other side is a complicated one. Both sides avowedly pursue a fundamental critique of domination and strive for the self-determination of all human beings. Hence, some postcolonial critics regularly make positive references to the \u201cuniversals enshrined in post-Enlightenment rationalism or humanism\u201d (Chakrabarty 2007, 70). Yet, the relationship gets complicated, mainly due to two factors. The first is the fact that the very same theoretical tradition that produced these universalisms has been entangled with colonial domination and its legitimation. Some of the very same normative theories that promote the universal self-determination of free and equal human beings are closely linked to anthropological theories of inequality that assert essential differences between human groups and thereby explicitly or implicitly contribute to the justification of unequal treatment and (colonial) domination. I will refer to the former as the discourse of emancipation and the latter as the discourse of inequality. One of the first steps the postcolonial critique of mainstream theory usually takes is to show that prominent theorists of the discourse of emancipation, such as John Locke, John Stuart Mill, or Immanuel Kant, also contributed to the discourse of inequality, proving that both discourses are entangled (Eze 1997a, 1997b; Mills 1999; McCarthy 2009, 25\u201327; Quijano 2007). Thus, the question arises: What consequences should this entanglement have for the way we read these historical works and their more recent successor theories? The second factor complicating the relationship between postcolonial critique and the universalist theories from the Western tradition is the ethnocentric and ignorant treatment of global heterogeneity within many of these theories. Two axes of heterogeneity are relevant here; each is associated with a geographical that should not be taken The to the of and among which the Western is one. to the postcolonial of this heterogeneity is the that among other an and process in which the Western tradition other or Quijano 2014). The global on the other to differences produced by The former colonial as as the that from their are to as the global the are to as the global heterogeneity should be in but is heterogeneity on both the one a heterogeneity of with a that and by but which continues to on the other a heterogeneity of produced by and its post- or neocolonial Both axes w